-- the engine's WASM opcode descriptor table, and the projection that writes it into
-- src/engine.duo.
--
--   duo run tools/opcodes.duo          -- project; rewrites src/engine.duo
--   duo run tools/opcodes.duo --check  -- fail if the projection is stale
--
-- WHY THIS EXISTS. it used to hard-code 170 distinct opcode numbers across
-- 241 dispatch predicates -- in the interpreter ladder AND, independently,
-- in the JIT's ALU_OPS / CMP_OPS tables. The 2026-08-06 handoff named that
-- exact failure ("hand-writing the rest across interpreter arms *and* JIT
-- emitters is the thing to avoid") and it happened anyway, because there was
-- nothing to hand-write *from* on the engine side: duo's canonical table
-- (lib/std/wasm/ward_mvp_opcodes.duo) stops at 63 ops and the engine needs 170.
--
-- So OPS below is the descriptor, and every opcode number the engine compiles
-- against is projected from it. An opcode number now appears ONCE in this
-- repo per fact, not once per consumer.
--
-- THIS TABLE IS NOT A SECOND SOURCE OF TRUTH. `_agree` checks every row that
-- lib/std/wasm/ward_mvp_opcodes.duo also carries against that file, parsed at
-- run time, and refuses to project on any disagreement. duo's canonical table
-- stays the authority for the 63 it holds; OPS extends it and is answerable
-- to it. That check is negative-controlled by `_selfcheck` -- a table that
-- cannot disagree is not a check.
--
-- MONOGLOT: this is .duo, not the .py that used to do this job (deleted
-- 2026-08-07 after it silently read a /tmp path that no longer existed) and
-- not the .zig generator that owns lib/std/wasm/.

-- {opcode, canonical wasm name}. Ordered by opcode; the projection preserves
-- this order, which is what makes re-running a no-op.
OPS = {
    {0, "unreachable"},
    {1, "nop"},
    {2, "block"},
    {3, "loop"},
    {4, "if"},
    {5, "else"},
    {11, "end"},
    {12, "br"},
    {13, "br_if"},
    {14, "br_table"},
    {15, "return"},
    {16, "call"},
    {17, "call_indirect"},
    {26, "drop"},
    {27, "select"},
    {32, "local.get"},
    {33, "local.set"},
    {34, "local.tee"},
    {35, "global.get"},
    {36, "global.set"},
    {37, "table.get"},
    {38, "table.set"},
    {40, "i32.load"},
    {41, "i64.load"},
    {42, "f32.load"},
    {43, "f64.load"},
    {44, "i32.load8_s"},
